[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hi there. I\'m Vic, builder evangelist at HyperAgent. &gt;&gt; And I\'m Alex from the HyperAgent marketing team. &gt;&gt; And we\'re here to talk about what\'s new in HyperAgent this week. We\'ve got some big updates to HyperAgent and we\'ll talk through teams, [music] how you can use agents that work together, how agents use memory together, and all of the'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different models we support at HyperAgent, plus a lot of updates you all have been asking for. Our first big update is teams and [music] team memories. You might know that you can use teams on HyperAgent and that means multiple agents can be part [music] of a team that multiple people can use'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together. Alex has created a couple agents like his sizzle agent which I can use whenever [music] I want as part of the team. And I\'ve created HyperDocs which Alex can use to understand what\'s going on in the [music] product and'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'document product features. And we can use those together in a shared space. They use shared skills. But now the biggest new thing they have is shared memories. [music] Here I am in my team and if I jump into my team memories, I can see all of the memories that the'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'team agents can pull from each other. So these are all shared. We all choose to share [music] these memories. We share things about demos. We share things about brand safety and our brand voice. So agents can be scoped to have their own memories and then [music] pull from'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the team memories. If I jump into a specific agent like HyperDocs, I can look at my agent\'s knowledge and see what it\'s allowed to see. It can auto-save memories. [music] It can look at memories from our team library. Here I can go and I can link our brand'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'guidelines or anything like that and it will always pull from there. And those are things that are general about the HyperAgent team, how we talk, how we work, how we say things. And then as well as it can pull from memories [music] it\'s created on its own that are'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'specific to its job. Your agents can pull and learn from the way your entire team works. They also can [music] pull on memories from how they work specifically to be more powerful on their own jobs as well [music] as powerful within the team.'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; My agent keeps track of its own memories and just have that auto-save on and it just accumulates all the little preferences and ticks that I have. Usually the feedback that I give it is what ends up in memories. &gt;&gt; And memories are great because they\'re'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] like little post-it notes of things to remember. So on my desktop and my screen I always have "Don\'t say um [music] in videos." That\'s the same thing for agents. They can pull little bits of context to make sure [music] that they\'re predictably responding, whereas the team skills that they have'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are shared processes that make sure that an entire process or an entire workflow is repeatable and predictable. [music] And now your agents can use team skills, so one skill shipped to a team is able to use, as well as those smaller'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memories and those smaller facts are also now being able to use by an entire team of agents. &gt;&gt; So, staying on this topic of a team of agents, we\'ve also introduced agent delegation. So, agents in HyperAgent don\'t work alone. Let me show you an example of how one agent can delegate to'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'others to get a job done. Here on the HyperAgent go-to-market team, we have an orchestrator agent called Maven, which is kind of like our chief of staff for all things marketing. And I was working with Maven to build out a landing page'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; AI agencies and consultants. Maven broke this job down into different tasks that are better suited to the specialists on the team. So, it allocated market'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'research work to an agent called Intel. It gave the copywriting work to an agent called Quill. [music] And then it asked Sizzle, which is our motion design agent, to build the motion'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'graphics. And then the orchestrator put it all together. So, this was the final deliverable of all those agents working together. So, you can see we\'ve got good skills and memories around things like HyperAgent brand style and brand voice. It stuck to all that. And then it built'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'these actual motion design assets, as well as crisp copywriting that is on point with our brand. So, a little bit meta here, but it\'s showing agent teams in a motion design asset in a landing page, and it did a really nice job with'

[03:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that. I can always inspect the sub-agent threads. And so, I can go from the main thread here with Maven into [music] what was happening with Intel as it was digging into the market research for'

[03:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this particular task. I can see what Quill was thinking about copywriting, and I can see [music] what Sizzle was doing around all things motion graphics and how it reported back to Maven with its finished [music] product. So, that\'s'

[03:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'agent delegation in action. &gt;&gt; And the way that you can set up agent delegation in your team is you go to your specific agent like Hyper Docs or any agent that you have and underneath [music] the autonomy pane, you can scroll down and see who this agent can'

[04:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'call. And you can either invoke all agents in the team so you don\'t have to pick specific ones or you can actually delegate to specific [music] agents and say these are only the agents that should be able to call and be a little bit more prescriptive of how [music]'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your agents delegate. It\'s up to you and how your team works, but you have that control now and the [music] agents will call each other based on what needs to get done and what agent specialty is present. The next thing that\'s newer in Hyperagent and might not be new for some'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of you [music] is all of the new models we support. Within the past month, we have switched to not only just offering [music] one family of models, but now we offer more than 20 models on Hyperagent and you can get started right from the opening pane with any model you choose.'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, you\'ll see here I have Kimmy 3 fast which we just supported last week, but you can jump into your thread settings, click over to model and see all the latest models including Fable, Opus 5, [music] GPT 5.6 Soul. And then if you scroll down, you can see all of our'

[04:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other providers including [music] Gemini, Fugu, Grok and Muse. I particularly like using Grok these days as well as all of the open weight models which [music] are significantly less expensive than some of the frontier models, but do a lot [music] of work'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around spurring on various small tasks like research, like drafting, like triaging and cataloging information. [music] All of those things can be done at a much lower price and now with agent delegation, some of your agents like'

[05:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your orchestrating agents and the ones that are telling your agents to do things and [music] delegating can be the more reasoning agents and try to figure out how should I break up these tasks? What should be done? What are all the pieces in this job? And then your sprinters [music] can go ahead and run on smaller models'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on those open source models saving you so much more money, but getting you so much more output as well. Go ahead and try in Hyperagent all the different models, see which ones you like for a specific job. For me, with my chief of staff agent who\'s now triaging my inbox'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or telling me what\'s new, I typically run that on a smaller model like GLM, whereas I\'ve been playing around a lot with Kimmy [music] as my daily driver for more strategic and reasoning work. And then when it\'s for the big guns and I\'m doing something really strategic and'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m sending it to my team and this is something that\'s going to get out to the public, [music] I\'m using Opus 5 or Fable when something requires a lot of design work. Now you can play with all those models right from within hyperagent [music] and not only can you actually start the'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thread, if you jump into an existing thread and you want to see how a different model would work, you can jump into [music] your activity, jump into a thread, and actually change it straight from the model header here. So you can see here I\'m using Muse. I want to go'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ahead and change that to GLM [music] fast and that\'ll change right there. I can also see the different costs when I go into my usage and see which models I\'ve been using. So give it a try and let us know which models you like and which models you want to see in'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hyperagent. &gt;&gt; Okay, a few more quick hits. Browser replays. Browser work now leaves a replay. So when an agent completes a session using its browser, you can see what it actually did. So you can open the file, play the saved video directly'

[06:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in the thread. So for example, maybe your agent is going through a purchasing workflow. You can verify that it used the supplier portal and the cart correctly before approving the purchase. So you can [music] actually watch back'

[07:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what your agent did in the browser. &gt;&gt; And we\'ve made hyperagent so much easier [music] to search with our improved search. So when you jump into the product, you can actually command K, look for your teams, [music] look for your memories, and you can even look for a specific thread that you\'ve been'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working on by just saying something like open source models, which is something I\'ve been working on. Or now I can see all the different threads [music] that also include that as well. So now you can search right from your command K. Also you can search with your agents. So we\'ve got a pretty big team of agents'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going on in our GTM team. [music] I can now search for that right off top. I can search for all my memories and it\'s a really cleaner way to navigate throughout hyperagent as you keep building out your agents, building out your contacts, and working in one single [music] platform. We also have the'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ability to put everybody into one cost center, so you\'ll get one invoice [music] for multiple people on your team, which is great for businesses that are now building out their agents and working as a team of agents in HyperAgent. So, you\'ll go to your [music] profile, go to settings, then'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can jump into billing, and you can see that you can create an organization where you have shared billing. So, my friends and I have a cake company together called Thunder Thighs Cake Co. We are all together on this one [music] team, and all of these agents and all of'

